        <div id="gauges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. 18 Gauge Brad vs 16 Gauge Finish vs 23 Gauge Pin
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nail gauge is a wire thickness number, and like wire, a higher gauge means a thinner nail. The three small nailers in a trim carpenter's kit are the 16 gauge finish nailer, the 18 gauge brad nailer and the 23 gauge pin nailer, and each does a different job.
          </p>

          <p>
            An 18 gauge brad is the middle ground and the most versatile. Brad nailers typically drive brads from 5/8 inch up to about 2 or 2-1/8 inches, enough to fasten 1/2 inch to 3/4 inch trim to studs and jambs. The brad is thin enough to rarely split narrow stock and leaves a small hole that fills easily. That is why it is the first nailer to buy for baseboard, door and window casing, shoe molding, cabinet face frames and furniture builds.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 16 gauge finish nail is thicker, usually with a slightly larger head, and finish nailers drive longer nails, often up to 2-1/2 inches. The extra diameter and length give real holding power for heavy casing, thick baseboard, stair parts and exterior trim, where an 18 gauge brad can pull through or bend. The trade-off is a larger hole and more risk of splitting thin or narrow stock. Some brands sell 16 and 18 gauge cordless nailers in near-identical bodies with similar model numbers, so check the gauge on any listing before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 23 gauge pin is so thin it is almost headless. Pins hold delicate pieces like small moldings, veneer and glued joints while the glue cures, and the hole is nearly invisible. Pins have little holding power on their own, so they are a glue-up clamp substitute, not a fastener.
          </p>

