                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to about 2-1/2 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy casing, thick baseboard, stair parts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bigger hole, can split thin stock</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">18 ga brad</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5/8 in. to about 2-1/8 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most interior trim, cabinets, furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited holding power in heavy trim</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">23 ga pin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short pins only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Delicate moldings, veneer, glue-ups</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost no holding power alone</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are buying one small nailer, buy the 18 gauge. It handles the widest range of indoor jobs. Add a 16 gauge when you start hanging solid wood doors or heavy casing, and a 23 gauge if you build furniture or install fine molding. For structural work, which none of these tools are designed for, see our <a href="/best-framing-nailer/">best framing nailer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-brads-do" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Brad Nailer Is For, and What It Isn't
          </h2>

          <p>
            A brad's job is mostly to hold. On baseboard and casing, the brad pins the trim tight to the wall or jamb so caulk and paint finish the joint. In furniture and cabinet work, brads hold glued parts in alignment while the glue cures, so you need fewer clamps. In both cases the long-term strength comes from the glue, the framing behind the drywall or the joinery, not the brad.
          </p>

          <p>
            That shapes how you use one. Shoot trim into studs and the top or bottom plate wherever possible, not just into drywall, which holds a brad poorly. Angle pairs of brads in opposing directions so they lock the piece in place. And where something will be pulled on, such as a stair skirt, a heavy shelf edge or a cabinet hung on a wall, use screws or a 16 gauge finish nailer instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            A brad nailer is also not a substitute for a framing nailer or for screws. Brads are not rated for structural loads, decks, fences or anything outdoors that will see weather. Exterior trim calls for corrosion-resistant fasteners and usually a heavier gauge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where a brad nailer really earns its keep is speed and cleanliness. Hand-nailing trim with a hammer and nail set takes a long time per brad and risks a hammer dent in finished wood. A brad nailer sets each fastener in one trigger pull, flush or just below the surface, with no dents. On a room of baseboard, that difference is hours.
          </p>
        </div>

