        <div id="cordless-vs-air" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cordless vs Pneumatic: The Real Trade-Off
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pneumatic brad nailers are lighter, simpler and cheaper. Metabo HPT lists the NT50AE2 at only 2.2 pounds, and Bostitch's BTFP12233 is listed at about 3.1 pounds. The cordless tools here are listed at roughly 5 to 7 pounds; DeWalt lists the DCN680 at about 5.3 pounds and Ryobi lists the P322 at 5.5 pounds, both before a battery is fitted. That difference is noticeable when you are holding a nailer at arm's length on a crown or casing job. Air tools also have very few parts to fail and, being simple mechanically, they fire instantly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost of air is the compressor and the hose. You need a compressor, a hose long enough to reach every room, fittings, and the noise of the compressor cycling. Both pneumatic picks here list an operating range of 70 to 120 psi. A small pancake or hot-dog compressor is commonly used for brad and finish nailers, because these tools use far less air per shot than framing nailers. If you already own a compressor, a pneumatic brad nailer is the best value in this category by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cordless brad nailers remove the compressor, hose and setup. You pick up the tool and go: one piece of shoe molding in a closet, a loose stair tread trim, a punch list in a finished home where dragging a hose over hardwood floors is a bad idea. For homeowners who do small trim jobs a few times a year, that convenience is worth the extra weight. For anyone who already owns batteries on a platform, the bare tool is often reasonably priced.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Already own a compressor? Buy air. Already own batteries? Buy the bare cordless tool.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The cheapest good brad nailer is the one that uses power you already have. If you own neither, cordless costs more up front but sets up in seconds; pneumatic is lighter and cheaper if you also want a compressor for tires and other air tools.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The same logic applies to framing nailers, where the weight gap is even larger; the <a href="/best-framing-nailer/">framing nailer guide</a> covers that comparison for heavy work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mechanisms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cordless Mechanisms: Flywheel vs Gas Spring
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cordless nailers do not contain a tiny compressor in the usual sense. They store energy in one of a few ways and release it to drive the brad, and the mechanism affects how quickly the tool fires and how it feels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flywheel designs spin up a heavy wheel with the motor, then clutch it to the driver blade to push the nail. DeWalt's DCN680 uses a flywheel. Reviewers describe a short delay before the first shot when the flywheel has been idle, then quick follow-up shots while it stays spun up. In practice the delay is brief, but you will notice it when nailing slowly and deliberately, one brad at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas spring designs use a sealed cylinder of compressed gas that the motor re-compresses after each shot. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL 2746-20 uses sealed compressed nitrogen and is advertised with zero ramp-up time and up to three nails per second. These tools fire when you pull the trigger, which feels closer to a pneumatic nailer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi's AirStrike tools are designed to work without a compressor or gas cartridge and are among the most affordable cordless options. Older fuel-cell nailers, which burn a gas cartridge, still exist but have been largely displaced in the brad category by battery tools that need no consumables.
          </p>

          <p>
            For trim work, any of these designs will do the job. The firing delay matters most if you work fast in bump mode or if you are used to the instant response of an air tool. If you are, put the Milwaukee or a pneumatic nailer at the top of your list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depth-nose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Depth Adjustment and Nose Design
          </h2>

          <p>
            Depth control is what separates a clean finish from a callback. The goal is to set each brad just below the surface so it can be filled, without the nose dimpling the wood. Hardwood trim like oak needs more drive than pine or MDF, and the same tool on the same setting will set nails differently in each.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick on this list has tool-free depth adjustment, usually a wheel or dial near the nose. Bostitch calls its system Dial-A-Depth. Get in the habit of firing a few test brads into scrap of the same material before you start on finished trim, and adjust until the head sits just below the surface. If you switch from pine baseboard to oak casing, test again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nose design matters for accuracy. A narrow nose lets you see exactly where the brad will go, which matters on narrow stock like shoe molding and on the thin edges of casing. DeWalt says its DCN680's micro nose improves line of sight compared with its older DC608. Bostitch's Smart Point nose is designed so you do not have to depress a large safety contact to fire, which helps with placement. Milwaukee's second-generation 2746-20 was redesigned with a slimmer nose than the original, according to reviewers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for a non-marring tip, a soft rubber or plastic pad on the contact. It protects prefinished trim and stained wood from the nose. Several kits include spare tips; Bostitch includes extra no-mar tips with the BTFP12233. Tool-free jam release also matters, because brads will occasionally jam, especially with cheap or damp-collated nails, and clearing a jam without hunting for a hex key saves time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lockout-modes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Dry-Fire Lockout and Firing Modes
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dry-fire lockout stops the tool from firing when the magazine is empty or nearly empty. Without it, the driver blade strikes the work with no nail in front of it, leaving a dent in the trim and, over time, wearing the driver. On cordless nailers it also prevents wasted battery charge. DeWalt describes its low-nail lockout as preventing dry firing and marks on the work; one hands-on review reported the DCN680 locks out with about 11 brads still in the magazine, which the reviewer felt was high. Milwaukee lists a dry-fire lockout on the 2746-20.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most pneumatic brad nailers do not include a lockout, so you need to watch the magazine. With a pneumatic, a quick glance at the magazine window before an important shot on finished trim is a good habit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Firing modes come in two types. Sequential mode fires one brad each time you press the nose to the work and pull the trigger. It is slower but more precise, and it is the right choice for finish work. Contact or bump mode fires each time the nose touches the work while the trigger is held. It is faster for long runs of hidden fastening, but it is also how double-fired brads and misplaced nails happen. The Milwaukee 2746-20 and Makita XNB04Z offer both modes.
          </p>

          <p>
            For trim, use sequential mode almost all the time. Bump mode is for production work on surfaces that will be covered, not for visible casing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nail-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Picking Brad Length for Trim
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brad length depends on the thickness of the trim and what is behind it. A common rule of thumb is that the brad should be about three times the thickness of the piece being fastened, so it passes through the trim and drywall and gets a solid bite in framing. In practice that works out to a few standard sizes.
          </p>

          <p>
            For 1/2 inch to 3/4 inch baseboard and casing over 1/2 inch drywall, 2 inch brads into studs and plates are the usual choice. For shoe molding and quarter round, shorter brads around 1-1/4 inches avoid hitting anything hidden behind the base. For cabinet face frames and furniture, size the brad to the thickness of the parts you are joining so the point does not break through the far side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to each tool's maximum. The DeWalt DCN680, Milwaukee 2746-20, Ryobi P322 and Bostitch BTFP12233 are listed for brads up to 2-1/8 inches, while the Metabo HPT NT50AE2 and Makita XNB04Z top out at 2 inches. That extra 1/8 inch rarely matters, but if you regularly install thick casing over plaster, buy a tool with the longer maximum or step up to a 16 gauge finish nailer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy quality brads from the same brand or a reputable fastener maker. Cheap brads with weak collation glue break apart in the magazine and cause jams, and slightly oversized brads can feed poorly. Galvanized brads resist rust for damp areas like bathrooms. Store them dry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Battery Platform, and Finishing the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cordless brad nailer's battery is its biggest hidden cost. Every cordless pick here except the Craftsman kit and DeWalt kit is listed as a bare tool, and the right choice usually comes down to which batteries you already own. A DeWalt nailer on a shelf of DeWalt packs costs only the tool price; the same tool for someone on Milwaukee means buying batteries and a charger too.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not own a platform yet, a combo kit is usually the cheapest way to get batteries and a charger, with a drill and driver you will use anyway. Our <a href="/best-cordless-tool-combo-kit/">best cordless tool combo kit guide</a> explains how to choose one, and <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> compares the two biggest platforms, both of which have excellent brad nailers. Ryobi owners should note that ONE+ packs fit the P322, and the HP battery is what Ryobi uses for its nails-per-charge rating; see <a href="/are-ryobi-one-plus-batteries-interchangeable/">are Ryobi ONE+ batteries interchangeable</a> for which packs work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery size matters less for a brad nailer than for a saw. Brads take little energy, and manufacturers' per-charge ratings run into the hundreds or thousands. Ryobi rates the P322 at up to 2,250 brads on a 4Ah ONE+ HP battery. A compact 2.0Ah pack keeps the tool lighter, which you will appreciate when nailing crown overhead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Finishing the job</h3>

          <p>
            A brad nailer is one part of a trim job. Clean, tight miters come from a good saw; see our <a href="/best-miter-saw/">best miter saw guide</a>. After nailing, fill the holes with a wood filler that sands and stains or paints well, which our <a href="/best-wood-filler/">best wood filler guide</a> covers, and caulk the gaps between trim and wall with a paintable caulk from our <a href="/best-paintable-caulk/">best paintable caulk guide</a>. A small hole set just below the surface, filled and painted, disappears.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using brads where finish nails belong</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An 18 gauge brad holds trim while glue or caulk does the long-term work. On heavy casing, thick baseboard, stair parts or anything that will be pulled on, brads can bend or pull through. Use a 16 gauge finish nailer or screws there, and keep brads for lighter trim and glue-ups.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the test shots</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Depth that is perfect in pine will leave heads proud in oak and blow through MDF. Fire a few brads into scrap of the same material before starting on finished trim and adjust the depth wheel until heads sit just below the surface. Test again every time the material changes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Nailing into drywall instead of framing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brads hold poorly in drywall alone. Locate studs and plates before installing baseboard and casing, and angle pairs of brads in opposing directions for grip. Trim that looks tight on day one can pull away from the wall within weeks when it is held only by gypsum.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cordless nailer on the wrong platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bare cordless nailer is only good value if you already own the batteries. Buying a bare tool on a new platform means paying for batteries and a charger too, which can cost more than the nailer. Buy on the platform you own, or buy a pneumatic if you already have a compressor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving bump mode on for finish work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Contact or bump mode is fast, but it is how double-fired brads and misplaced nails end up in visible trim. Use sequential mode for anything that will be seen, and save bump mode for fast fastening on surfaces that will be covered. Lock the trigger or remove the battery when moving around.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

