          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best brad nailer for most people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, the DeWalt DCN680 is the best all-round cordless brad nailer: it drives 5/8 to 2-1/8 inch brads, has a micro nose for accurate placement, tool-free depth adjustment and a low-nail lockout. If you already own a compressor, the Metabo HPT NT50AE2 does the same job for less money and at 2.2 pounds is far lighter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cordless or pneumatic brad nailer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pneumatic brad nailers are lighter, cheaper and fire instantly, but need a compressor and hose. Cordless nailers are heavier and cost more, but you can pick one up and nail one piece of trim in seconds. Buy pneumatic if you own a compressor, cordless if you own batteries on a platform with a good nailer, and choose based on convenience if you own neither.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between an 18 gauge and 16 gauge nailer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An 18 gauge brad is thinner, leaves a smaller hole and is less likely to split narrow trim, but it has less holding power. A 16 gauge finish nail is thicker and usually longer, holding heavy casing, thick baseboard and stair parts better. Most people should buy an 18 gauge first and add a 16 gauge for heavy trim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best brad nailer for trim?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For trim, look for a narrow nose, a non-marring tip, tool-free depth adjustment and sequential firing. The DeWalt DCN680 and Milwaukee 2746-20 are the best cordless picks, and the Bostitch BTFP12233 with its Smart Point nose is a great pneumatic choice. Use 2 inch brads into studs for baseboard and casing, and shorter brads for shoe molding.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should brad nails be for baseboard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For 1/2 to 3/4 inch baseboard over 1/2 inch drywall, 2 inch brads are the usual choice, long enough to pass through the trim and drywall and grip the studs and bottom plate. For shoe molding and quarter round, around 1-1/4 inch brads avoid hitting anything behind the baseboard. A common rule of thumb is a brad about three times the trim thickness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size compressor do I need for a brad nailer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small pancake or hot-dog compressor is commonly used for 18 gauge brad nailers, because they use far less air per shot than framing nailers. Check the operating pressure on the nailer; both pneumatic picks here list 70 to 120 psi. A larger compressor will run less often but is not necessary for trim work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a brad nailer be used for framing or decks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Brads are thin, are not rated for structural loads and do not belong in framing, decks or fences. Exterior work needs corrosion-resistant fasteners and heavier nails or screws. For structural work, use a framing nailer; our framing nailer guide compares cordless, pneumatic and fuel-cell options for that job.
              </p>
